---
/* Rich body for the "Designing a support chat experience" case
   study (Namecheap). The shared [slug].astro template renders
   this when the slug matches; the default templated layout is
   bypassed. Shared .rwc-* layout/typography lives in global.css
   and the image lightbox in CaseBodyLightbox.astro. */
import CaseBodyLightbox from './CaseBodyLightbox.astro';
---

<div class="rwc-body">

  {/* ───────────────────── HERO / CONTEXT ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-hero" id="case-context">
    <div class="rwc-hero-left">
      <div class="case-cat">Case study · Platform for Namecheap's customer support</div>
      <h1 class="case-title">Designing a support chat experience</h1>
      <p class="case-subtitle">
        One of the biggest domain providers in the world, driven to pursue
        an amazing customer experience, challenged me to build an in-house
        customer support app.
      </p>

      <div class="rwc-callout">
        <div class="rwc-callout-title">
          <span class="rwc-callout-star" aria-hidden="true">★</span>
          Core achievements
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Designed a customer support app and widget from scratch,
            learning from its users, and creating its own design system.
          </li>
          <li>
            Achieved a 97.4% positive feedback rate just 4 months after
            release, on the new chat and support experience.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rwc-hero-right">
      <img class="rwc-hero-mark" src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/hero-mark.svg" alt="Namecheap mark" loading="eager" />
      <div class="case-img-ph rwc-hero-img is-filled" data-ratio="4-3">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/hero.webp" alt="Namecheap branding — Building a better online world together" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
      </div>
      <p class="case-img-caption">Image by Namecheap via namecheap.com</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── MY ROLE + KEYWORDS ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-role" id="case-role">
    <div class="rwc-role-left">
      <div class="eyebrow">My role</div>
      <h2 class="rwc-h2">UX Designer</h2>

      <p>
        I led the customer support app design from its start, created a
        design system to support it, ran research with customer support
        users, and was responsible for the whole design process, from
        discovery to delivery.
      </p>

      <dl class="rwc-aside">
        <div>
          <dt>My responsibilities</dt>
          <dd>Lead designer, front-end code reviewer</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>The team</dt>
          <dd>Lead designer (myself), 7 engineers, 1 product manager, 1 engineering manager, 8 engineers</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Duration</dt>
          <dd>10 months</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <aside class="rwc-role-right">
      <div class="rwc-initiatives-title">Keywords</div>
      <ul class="rwc-initiatives-list">
        <li>Customer support engagement</li>
        <li>Prototyping</li>
        <li>Motion</li>
        <li>Field research</li>
        <li>Multistreaming</li>
        <li>Built a design system</li>
        <li>Designed product from its inception</li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── THE CHALLENGE ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-challenge">
    <div class="rwc-challenge">
      <div class="rwc-challenge-text">
        <div class="eyebrow">The challenge</div>
        <h2 class="rwc-h2">Customer support chat application</h2>
        <p>
          The challenge was to create an in-house customer support
          application with multichannel capabilities. Namecheap's customer
          support teams were using a dated app, and it didn't fit their
          needs. We started by creating a chat + bot, along with a CS agent
          application, while learning from in-house customer support agents.
        </p>

        <p class="rwc-currently-live">
          <strong>Currently live at:</strong><br/>
          <a href="https://www.namecheap.com/help-center/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Namecheap's Help Center</a><br/>
          <a href="https://www.spaceship.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spaceship</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rwc-challenge-img">
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="3-4">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/chat-widget-feedback.webp" alt="Chat widget with Your Feedback Matters thumbs prompt" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── KICKOFF AND RESEARCH ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-kickoff">
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">Kickoff and research</h2>
    <p class="rwc-lead">
      The project kicked off with a clear vision, communicated to me and
      the engineering lead by the existing customer support leads. We
      started by interviewing them to learn as much as we could about
      their process, so we could design a v1 that met their needs and
      provided the structure to scale.
    </p>

    <div class="rwc-columns cols-3">
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="1-1">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/shadowing.webp" alt="Shadowing a Namecheap customer support agent at their desk in Ukraine" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <h3>Shadowing + interviews</h3>
        <p>
          Went to Namecheap's Customer Support HQ in Ukraine to meet the
          agents, supervisors, and managers, and understand how they work
          daily.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="1-1">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/experience-canvas.webp" alt="Experience canvas template — hypothesis, problems, value, users, MVP, success metrics" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <h3>Built an experience canvas</h3>
        <p>
          Used all the input I got from the customer support teams to
          build an experience canvas, as a blueprint to kickstart the
          project.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="1-1">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/competitive-benchmark.webp" alt="Competitive support tool — VIP inbox, conversations, and reply panel" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <h3>Competitive benchmark</h3>
        <p>
          Identified possible product competitors and highlighted what
          makes each one stand out, gaps, and benefits.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── WHAT WE DISCOVERED ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-discovery">
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">What we discovered</h2>

    <div class="rwc-discovery">
      <ul class="rwc-bullets">
        <li>
          Agents had inefficient shifts, required an extremely high amount
          of training to learn 20+ different tools, and their current
          tool, Kayako, wasn't optimized to their exact needs.
        </li>
        <li>
          Support agents had to handle more than 5 chats at once, while
          they had 20+ browser tabs open — each with a different, specific
          tool to solve customer problems.
        </li>
        <li>
          Clients reaching out to customer support had difficulty
          accessing the chat quickly without their account info.
          Engagement feedback at the end of the chat was low, which led
          to poor NPS scores.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="rwc-goal">
        <p class="rwc-goal-eyebrow">Our goal was clear:</p>
        <h3 class="rwc-goal-title">
          To build an integrated solution for support agents and customers.
        </h3>
        <ul class="rwc-bullets">
          <li>
            By increasing agent satisfaction and the ease-of-use of the
            customer support engagement software, when compared to their
            current toolset.
          </li>
          <li>
            By increasing the positive feedback received overall from
            clients, and the absolute number of chats receiving feedback,
            contributing to an increased NPS score.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── LOW-FI / HIGH-FI / TESTS ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-design-iterations">
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">Low-fi, High-fi, Unmoderated tests</h2>

    <div class="rwc-sketch-grid">
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="4-3">
        <img class="rwc-flip-v" src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/sketch-ui-notes.webp" alt="Sketchbook: UI notes into messages — chat bubble layouts" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled rwc-rotated-tile" data-ratio="1-1">
        <img class="rwc-rotate-90" src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/sketch-arrangement.webp" alt="Sketchbook: arrangement notes and admin report wireframes" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="4-3">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/sketch-bubble-variants.webp" alt="Sketchbook: chat bubble variants — T, T+A, T+A+Expand" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rwc-columns cols-2">
      <p>
        Working out the arrangement between chat bubbles, and
        distinguishing received from sent ones, just by using layout. Also
        considering each message's specific actions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Wireframes to map out the variants of the chat bubble. T - Just
        text / T+A - Text and attachment / T+A+Expand - All the previous
        but containing a huge text message (allowing collapse/expand).
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* System blueprints / User flows */}
    <div class="rwc-subsection">
      <h3 class="rwc-h3">System blueprints / User flows</h3>
      <p>
        This system blueprint + flow to tackle alerts on
        disconnect/auth-error/general errors was a big part of making sure
        the system provided the right UX at such critical moments.
      </p>
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-9">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/system-blueprint.webp" alt="System blueprint and user flow tackling disconnect, auth-error, and general-error alerts" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
    </div>

    {/* High-fi prototypes */}
    <div class="rwc-subsection">
      <h3 class="rwc-h3">High-fi prototypes</h3>
      <p>
        Moved on to high-fidelity and motion design, where we aimed to
        provide an easy-going appearance to the chat, with a neutral
        tone-of-voice.
      </p>

      <div class="rwc-columns cols-3">
        <div class="rwc-column">
          <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="3-4">
            <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/floating-button-states.webp" alt="Floating button — hover, default, agent avatar variants for Storybook Clients and Agents" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="rwc-caption">
            Scheme of the floating button states (above), and a complete
            view of the widget opened with an ongoing conversation.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rwc-column">
          <div class="rwc-error-stack">
            <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="1-1">
              <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/error-503.webp" alt="503 error illustration — ladder, wrench, and pile in a hole" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
            <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="1-1">
              <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/error-404.webp" alt="404 error illustration — ladder and magnifying glass over a hole" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="rwc-caption">Illustrations for errors.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rwc-column">
          <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="3-4">
            <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/chat-widget.webp" alt="High-fidelity chat widget mockup — Jane Doe Available" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── CUSTOMER-FACING CHAT ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-customer-chat">
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">The customer-facing chat interface.</h2>

    <div class="rwc-annotated">
      <ul class="rwc-annotated-list">
        <li>
          We opted for a hovering widget that could be there as the user
          is browsing, unlike Kayako, which used a native OS window
          separate from the main browser one.
        </li>
        <li>We made it themable to any other brand requirements.</li>
        <li>
          Avatars only included for agents' messages, as users don't have
          a profile picture.
        </li>
        <li>
          Floating button included an animated feature to convey the look
          &amp; feel we wanted.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled rwc-annotated-img" data-ratio="3-4">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/chat-widget.webp" alt="Annotated customer-facing chat widget" loading="lazy" />
      </div>

      <ul class="rwc-annotated-list">
        <li>
          Visibility of system status is provided on the header, on both
          agent status and if errors occur.
        </li>
        <li>
          If colorblind, the user can still scan the message author based
          on bubble grid alignment.
        </li>
        <li>Messages stacked if sent within the same sequence (10 seconds).</li>
        <li>
          Main input field will reveal additional controls if focused, or
          if it contains files ready to be sent (progressive disclosure).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── BUILDING A DESIGN SYSTEM ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-design-system">
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">Building a design system</h2>
    <h3 class="rwc-h3">From foundation and principles to styleguide and components.</h3>
    <p>
      The chat was intended to be a white-label product, so we considered
      different themes to kick off its system. We did 2 themes to start
      with, for namecheap.com and ssls.com.
    </p>

    {/* Restructured: button docs (the largest piece of evidence) is
       a full-width visual; the two palettes sit as equal-weight
       tiles below, instead of being squashed alongside it. */}
    <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
      <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/button-docs.webp" alt="Button component documentation — variants, sizes, color classes, states" loading="lazy" />
    </div>

    <p class="case-img-caption">
      Our documentation on Sketch was extensive and kept by myself, and
      synced with the Storybook front-end repositories.
    </p>

    <div class="rwc-columns cols-2">
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <h3>Status tokens</h3>
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="3-4">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/status-colors.webp" alt="Status colors — Success, Warning, Danger, Info tokens at 10/40/50 stops" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <h3>Per-brand palette</h3>
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="3-4">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/ssls-colors.webp" alt="SSLs.com primary color palette — sep-ssl tokens" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rwc-columns cols-2">
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <h3>Component variants</h3>
        <p>
          We mapped and created components to feed the system as the
          product needs and features evolved. (Alert component above.)
        </p>
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="3-4">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/alert-component.webp" alt="Alert component variants — default, danger, warning, success, info" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <h3>Component anatomy</h3>
        <p>
          Each component had a description of its anatomy and composition.
        </p>
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="3-4">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/floating-button-anatomy.webp" alt="Floating button anatomy — background, optional badge, content slots" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rwc-subsection">
      <h3 class="rwc-h3">Web accessibility</h3>
      <p>
        To match WCAG guidelines, we applied contrast compliance (A, AA),
        focus ordering details, and focus states for keyboard and speech
        navigation.
      </p>
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-9">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/accessibility.webp" alt="Web accessibility examples — feedback form, focus order, focus outline on keyboard press" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── OUTCOMES & IMPACT ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-impact">
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">Outcomes and impact</h2>

    <div class="rwc-stats">
      <div class="rwc-stat">
        <div class="rwc-stat-n">28.9<span class="rwc-stat-unit">%</span></div>
        <div class="rwc-stat-desc">
          Increase in customer engagement chats for the same period. From
          15k using Kayako, to 21k, in 4 months.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-stat">
        <div class="rwc-stat-n">4800<span class="rwc-stat-unit">+</span></div>
        <div class="rwc-stat-desc">
          Chats received feedback — an increase of over 15% compared to
          the same period before launch.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-stat">
        <div class="rwc-stat-n">97.4<span class="rwc-stat-unit">%</span></div>
        <div class="rwc-stat-desc">
          Positive feedback to all chats, on the new chat and support
          experience.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      We kept track of metrics such as monthly engagements, monthly
      feedback rates, and feedback reports.
    </p>

    {/* Not everything was perfect */}
    <div class="rwc-subsection">
      <h3 class="rwc-h3">Not everything was perfect, though.</h3>
      <div class="rwc-challenge">
        <div class="rwc-challenge-text">
          <p>
            We detected an agent-side platform navigation inefficiency,
            which we acknowledged after following up with our agents. We
            adjusted the navigation, tested it, and after 2 weeks we found
            a solution our agents were happy with.
          </p>
          <p>
            The revamped navigation for the agents' platform achieved a
            4.6 out of 5 satisfaction rating with the newly proposed
            navigation, as well as a higher preference compared to the
            first release, giving us the confidence to move forward with
            it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rwc-challenge-img">
          <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
            <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/preference-survey.webp" alt="Preference survey results between the new and previous navigation" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
            <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/satisfaction-chart.webp" alt="Satisfaction emoji chart — 0%, 0%, 15%, 44%, 41%" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── CURRENT PRODUCT STATE ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-current-state">
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">Current product state</h2>
    <p>
      The Engagement.AI chat is currently available on both
      <a href="https://www.namecheap.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">namecheap.com</a>
      and
      <a href="https://www.spaceship.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">spaceship.com</a>.
      It's themed according to each of the target brands.
    </p>

    <div class="rwc-columns cols-3">
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="3-4">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/floating-motion.gif" alt="Floating button and chat window motion in the live product" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <p class="rwc-caption">Floating button and chat window motion.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="3-4">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/chat-widget-feedback.webp" alt="Live product — new feedback form" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <p class="rwc-caption">New feedback form.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="3-4">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/namecheap/live-chat.gif" alt="Live product — chat widget menu with Namecheap Engagement.AI agent" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <p class="rwc-caption">Chat widget menu.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── RETROSPECTIVE ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-retrospective">
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">Retrospective</h2>

    <div class="rwc-columns cols-2">
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <h3>What went well</h3>
        <p>
          Great collaboration with the engineering team led to great
          results and feedback from both users and customer support
          agents.
        </p>
        <p>
          Front-end and design collaboration effectiveness and efficiency
          was consistently improved over time. We gradually refined our
          design QA process.
        </p>
        <p>
          Having refined the process as a team showed its benefits — at
          the later stages we were an oiled machine in delivering value.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-column">
        <h3>What could've been better</h3>
        <p>
          Even though it was an in-house project, reaching users with
          enough time to do research wasn't always easy.
        </p>
        <p>Technical delays held the project back in schedule.</p>
        <p>
          It was a great challenge to create such a huge product from
          scratch — but we managed to plan accordingly and we caught on,
          especially by having managed to be close to our users early on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>

<CaseBodyLightbox />

<style>
  /* DSC-specific overrides — shared .rwc-* layout/typography lives
     in global.css and the lightbox in CaseBodyLightbox.astro. */

  /* Filled tile drops the 180px floor: when an image is present, the
     aspect-ratio frame drives height instead of forcing sibling
     tiles of different ratios to a uniform tall row. */
  .case-img-ph.is-filled {
    min-height: 0;
    height: fit-content;
    align-self: start;
  }

  /* Slightly tighter caption rhythm and a contrasted body-link color. */
  .rwc-body a { color: var(--text-brand); }
  .rwc-caption { margin-top: var(--dimension-space-8); }

  /* Tinted section wash for the post-launch / impact band. */
  .rwc-section-tinted {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, #4ec1e0 12%, var(--surface-default));
    border-radius: var(--dimension-radius-lg, 12px);
    padding-left: var(--dimension-space-32);
    padding-right: var(--dimension-space-32);
  }

  /* Hero right column shrinks to its content — the banner sits at
     its natural size and the Namecheap mark above acts as a small
     fixed badge instead of stretching to the row. */
  .rwc-hero-right {
    align-self: start;
    height: fit-content;
  }
  .rwc-hero-right .case-img-ph.rwc-hero-img.is-filled {
    width: auto;
    min-height: 0;
  }
  .rwc-hero-right .rwc-hero-img img {
    height: auto;
    align-self: flex-start;
  }
  .rwc-hero-mark {
    display: block;
    width: var(--dimension-space-80, 80px);
    height: var(--dimension-size-44, 44px);
    margin: 0 auto var(--dimension-space-32);
  }

  /* Subtler callout wash than the global default — the border carries
     most of the visual weight here so the callout reads as grouped
     without sitting on a solid block of color. */
  .rwc-callout {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--text-primary) 4%, var(--surface-default));
  }

  /* Challenge / 2-col text+image. */
  .rwc-challenge {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.2fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--dimension-space-48);
    align-items: start;
    height: auto;
  }
  .rwc-challenge-img { display: grid; gap: var(--dimension-space-16); align-items: start; }
  .rwc-currently-live { font-size: var(--font-size-sm); margin-top: var(--dimension-space-24); }

  /* Discovery + columns: items align to top so a tall photo doesn't
     stretch its shorter neighbor; column headings get a slight bump. */
  .rwc-columns { align-items: start; }
  .rwc-columns h3 {
    margin-top: var(--dimension-space-16) !important;
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg) !important;
  }

